{"id":"aa00172e-502f-4f17-8c1e-c6524998f153","object":"chat.completion","created":1784884971,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\n    trials = case['trials']\\n    failures = {}\\n    successes = []\\n    for i, trial in enumerate(trials):\\n        entry = {\\n            'last_trial': i,\\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\\n            'success': trial['success']\\n        }\\n        if not trial['success']:\\n            failures[trial['signature']] = entry\\n        else:\\n            successes.append(entry)\\n    failure_list = list(failures.values())\\n    failure_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\\n    success_list = successes[:]\\n    success_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\\n    combined = failure_list + success_list\\n    retained = combined[:capacity]\\n    retained.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\\n    memory = retained\\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in retained]\\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task: AGENT-RF-01. The solve(case) function receives a payload object with \"capacity\" and \"trials\". It must return an object with \"memory\" (array of objects each with last_trial (integer), reflection (string), signature (string), success (boolean)) and \"signatures\" (array of strings). The process: deduplicate by failure signature (but note dedup by failure signature? The description: \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" This likely means if there are multiple entries with the same signature (maybe only failures? or all?), keep the newest (by trial index). Then, when capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature. Then restore chronological order in final memory.\n\nLet's parse the instructions more carefully:\n\n- Process trials in chronological order.\n- Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection. (Note: \"failure signature\" might mean the signature of a trial that was a failure? Or signature of any trial? The term \"failure signature\" likely means the signature of a trial that had success = false. But then \"retaining the newest reflection\" implies we only keep one entry per signature among failures? Or per signature overall? The phrase \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature\" suggests we only consider entries with success = false when deduplicating? Possibly we maintain a list of memory entries from trials that are failures, but we want to keep only the newest reflection for each signature among failures. However, the output includes both successes and failures? The output memory includes entries from trials that are retained, which can be successes or failures. The capacity prioritization says \"prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" So we need to select a subset of trials to keep, up to capacity. The first step might be: for each trial, we create a memory entry (with last_trial = the 0-based index? Or 1-based? The examples: In the first case, capacity=3, trials: 7 trials. The output memory will contain at most 3 entries. We need to decide which ones.\n\nLet's look at the sample cases? We don't have expected outputs, only input. But we can reason from the description.\n\nProcedure: \"Process trials in chronological order.\" So we iterate trials in order (index i from 0). For each trial, we consider it as a potential memory entry.\n\n\"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" This likely means while processing, if we encounter a failure (success=false) with a signature that already exists in the memory (from a previous failure entry), we should replace the earlier failure entry with the current one (since it's newer). In other words, for failures, we keep only the latest occurrence of each signature. For successes, maybe no deduplication? Or dedup applies to all? But it says \"by failure signature\", which implies only failures. If a signature appears in both successes and failures, we might need to handle separately.\n\nThen: \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" After processing all trials, we have a set of memory entries (some from failures, some from successes). Then we need to truncate to capacity by sorting: first all failures should be kept before any successes. Among failures, sort by recency (most recent? or earliest? \"then recency and signature\" - likely descending recency, and then signature as tiebreaker). Then among successes, similarly. Then take the top 'capacity' entries. Finally, \"restore chronological order in the final memory\" means sort the retained entries by trial index ascending.\n\nAlso, \"Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\" So output memory is the list of retained entries in chronological order, and signatures is a list of the signatures of those entries (in same order? Possibly just the set? But contract says \"signatures\" array of strings, likely in same order as memory entries? Probably yes: each memory entry's signature appears in signatures array, in corresponding order.\n\nAlso note: \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: feedback_provenance, memory_bound, stable_order, no_weight_update.\" These are likely just constraints that we must follow: memory_bound (capacity), stable_order (chronological order after truncation), no_weight_update (we are not updating any model weights).\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Initialize empty list memory_entries (chronological order as we process).\n2. For each trial index i (0-based), with fields signature, reflection, success:\n   - Create entry with last_trial = i (integer), reflection, signature, success.\n   - If success is False: deduplicate by signature. That means, if there exists an existing entry in memory_entries with same signature and success=False, replace that entry with this new one (or remove old and add new). But careful: if we replace, we might lose chronological order? Since we later will truncate and then re-sort. So we can maintain a dict for failures to keep the latest index per signature. But we also need to keep all successes? Possibly we need to keep all successes initially, then apply capacity. However, \"deduplicate memory by failure signature\" suggests that we should only keep one failure per signature. So we should update that failure entry when a newer failure with same signature appears. For successes, we keep all? But then capacity may limit. Also note the phrase \"retaining the newest reflection.\" That suggests for failures, we want the latest reflection for each failure signature.\n\nThus we can process trials, maintain a list or dict for failures such that for each signature, we keep the latest occurrence. For successes, we simply accumulate all.\n\nHowever, what about a trial that was a success but later a failure with same signature? Should the success be replaced? Possibly not, because deduplication is only \"by failure signature\", so successes are not deduplicated. But then when prioritizing failures before successes, the failure entry for that signature would be kept over the success? But the success entry might be newer? Actually, the deduplication step only applies to failures, so the failure entry exists separately from success entries with same signature. Then in capacity prioritization, failures are prioritized, so if we have both a failure and a success with same signature, the failure will be kept first, possibly pushing out the success. That seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to test with the given fixtures? Not given expected outputs. We'll implement accordingly.\n\nAlternative interpretation: maybe \"deduplicate memory by failure signature\" means we consider only failures when deciding which entries to keep, but we still keep successes as well. But then \"retaining the newest reflection\" for each failure signature. So after processing all trials, we have a set of failures (one per unique failure signature) and a set of all successes. Then apply capacity bound: keep all failures first (sorted by recency descending? Then by signature? Actually \"then recency and signature\" likely means when ties or ordering, use recency and signature as tiebreakers. But since we have to truncate to capacity, we need to select the top entries. The priority is: failures before successes. So we sort failures by some criteria, successes by some criteria. Then concatenate sorted failures and sorted successes, take first 'capacity'. Then re-sort by trial index ascending.\n\nWhat criteria for sorting failures and successes? \"then recency and signature.\" For failures, sort by recency descending (most recent first) because more recent failures are more important? Or ascending? The phrase \"prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" Could mean: within failures, sort by recency (most recent highest priority) and then signature as tiebreaker. Also for successes similarly. But we need to infer from typical memory or from the fact that during dedup we keep newest failure per signature. That suggests newer failures are more important. So within failures, sort by last_trial descending (most recent first). Then for successes, also descending.\n\nThen take top capacity.\n\nFinally, sort retained entries by last_trial ascending to restore chronological order.\n\nNow, what about \"signatures\" output? Should be the list of signatures from the final memory entries in order.\n\nLet's implement.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Input: case object has 'capacity' (int) and 'trials' (list of objects with signature, reflection, success).\n- Process trials with index.\n- Maintain a dict 'failures' mapping signature to entry (latest one). For each trial with success false: update failures[signature] = entry (overwrite previous).\n- Maintain a list 'successes' of all success entries (including duplicates).\n- After all trials, combine failures_entries = list of failures.values() (these are latest for each failure signature). successes_entries = list of all successes.\n- Sort failures_entries by last_trial descending (most recent first). Sort successes_entries by last_trial descending.\n- Concatenate sorted failures then sorted successes.\n- Truncate to capacity.\n- Sort the truncated list by last_trial ascending.\n- Build memory list as these objects.\n- Build signatures list as [entry['signature'] for entry in memory].\n- Return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}.\n\nBut need to check: Should we also consider that a failure could be replaced by a later success? No, dedup only for failures. Also, what about if a signature appears only in successes? No dedup for successes, so multiple entries may exist. Then capacity bound may remove some.\n\nPotential edge: capacity may be less than number of failures? Then we keep only the most recent failures (by recency) because they are sorted descending. That seems correct.\n\nLet's test mentally with first case: capacity=3, trials:\n0: sig e0, r0, success true -> success\n1: e3, r1, true -> success\n2: e2, r2, true -> success\n3: e1, r3, false -> failure, dict[e1] = entry(i=3)\n4: e1, r4, true -> success (note same signature as failure but success)\n5: e1, r5, false -> failure, dict[e1] = entry(i=5) (overwrites)\n6: e1, r6, false -> failure, dict[e1] = entry(i=6) (overwrites)\n\nFailures after all: one entry: e1 with last_trial=6, reflection=r6, success=false.\nSuccesses: indices 0,1,2,4 (4 successes).\nSort failures: [entry6] (most recent is only)\nSort successes: descending by last_trial: index4 (e1,r4,true), then index2 (e2,r2,true), index1 (e3,r1,true), index0 (e0,r0,true)\nConcatenated: [fail6, suc4, suc2, suc1, suc0] (actually after failures, then successes sorted descending)\nTruncate to 3: [fail6, suc4, suc2]\nThese are: e1 (failure, last_trial=6), e1 (success, last_trial=4), e2 (success, last_trial=2)\nNow sort ascending by last_trial: index2 (e2), index4 (e1 success), index6 (e1 failure) -> order: last_trial=2,4,6.\nMemory entries: \n- {last_trial:2, reflection:'r2', signature:'e2', success:true}\n- {last_trial:4, reflection:'r4', signature:'e1', success:true}\n- {last_trial:6, reflection:'r6', signature:'e1', success:false}\nSignatures: ['e2','e1','e1'].\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nBut is it correct to keep both a failure and a success with same signature? Possibly not; maybe the dedup by failure signature also implies that if a failure exists, it replaces any earlier success with same signature? The phrase \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection\" might mean that we should only keep the most recent entry for each signature, and if that entry is a failure, it overrides any previous successes? But it says \"by failure signature\", not \"by signature\". Let's read: \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" This could mean: For each signature that has at least one failure, keep only the newest failure (i.e., remove all but the most recent failure for that signature). It doesn't talk about removing successes. So successes are unaffected.\n\nHowever, note that later in prioritization, failures are prioritized before successes. So if we have both a success and a failure for same signature, the failure will be kept first, possibly pushing out the success. That's fine.\n\nBut consider another interpretation: maybe we should only keep entries that are failures? Because the phrase \"failure signature\" could imply we only store failures in memory? But the output schema includes success boolean, so we can store successes too. And the prioritization says \"prioritize failures before successes\", implying both are possible. So we keep both.\n\nThus the algorithm stands.\n\nOne more nuance: \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" The phrase \"then recency and signature\" could be applied to both failures and successes together, i.e., sort all entries by (is_failure? 0:1, -last_trial, signature) and take top capacity. But that is equivalent to sorting failures first, and within each group by recency descending, and within same recency? But last_trial is unique? Not necessarily, but could be multiple trials with same index? No, each trial has unique index. So recency is unique. But signature might be used as secondary sort if recency equal? Not possible. So it's fine.\n\nOur approach of separate sorts and concatenation matches that.\n\nNow implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nReturn format: JSON object with 'memory' and 'signatures'. Ensure all fields present.\n\nWrite Python code.\n\nConstraints: only standard library, no randomness, no file access, etc.\n\nNow produce the JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a string, properly escaped.\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we'll create an object with that key, and its value is a JSON string containing the Python source.\n\nLet's write the Python function.\n\nPotential edge: case may have empty trials? Then memory empty, signatures empty.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    capacity = case['capacity']\n    trials = case['trials']\n    failures = {}  # signature -> entry\n    successes = []\n    for i, trial in enumerate(trials):\n        entry = {\n            'last_trial': i,\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\n            'success': trial['success']\n        }\n        if not trial['success']:\n            # failure\n            sig = trial['signature']\n            failures[sig] = entry  # overwrite with newer\n        else:\n            successes.append(entry)\n    # combine\n    failure_list = list(failures.values())\n    # sort failures by last_trial descending\n    failure_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\n    success_list = successes\n    success_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\n    # concatenate\n    combined = failure_list + success_list\n    # truncate to capacity\n    retained = combined[:capacity]\n    # restore chronological order\n    retained.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\n    # build memory and signatures\n    memory = retained\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in retained]\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}\n\nBut note: In the deduplication, we overwrite the failure entry with the latest one. However, what about the case where we have multiple successes for same signature? They remain as separate entries. That seems correct.\n\nBut we must ensure that we don't exceed capacity by keeping more than capacity failures? The truncation after sorting ensures we keep at most capacity.\n\nPotential issues: if capacity is larger than total entries, we keep all.\n\nNow produce the JSON with implementation string. Need to escape newlines and quotes.\n\nWe'll write the code as a triple-quoted string inside the JSON. But the JSON value must be a string, so we escape accordingly.\n\nFor safety, we'll produce the JSON manually.\n\nLet's write the Python code as a string:\n\ncode = '''\ndef solve(case):\n    capacity = case['capacity']\n    trials = case['trials']\n    failures = {}\n    successes = []\n    for i, trial in enumerate(trials):\n        entry = {\n            'last_trial': i,\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\n            'success': trial['success']\n        }\n        if not trial['success']:\n            failures[trial['signature']] = entry\n        else:\n            successes.append(entry)\n    failure_list = list(failures.values())\n    failure_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\n    success_list = successes[:]\n    success_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\n    combined = failure_list + success_list\n    retained = combined[:capacity]\n    retained.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\n    memory = retained\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in retained]\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}\n'''\n\nBut we must ensure no mutable default arguments, etc. Fine.\n\nNow embed in JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\n    ...\"}\n\nWe need to escape backslashes, newlines, quotes. In Python, we can use json.dumps. But we are writing the JSON manually. Let's just produce the escaped string.\n\nWe'll write a response with the exact JSON.\n\nTo avoid mistakes, we can output the JSON string using Python script (not here). But since we need to respond directly, we'll manually escape.\n\nWe'll write the implementation string with double quotes and escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\" etc.\n\nLet's do it step by step.\n\nThe code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    capacity = case['capacity']\n    trials = case['trials']\n    failures = {}\n    successes = []\n    for i, trial in enumerate(trials):\n        entry = {\n            'last_trial': i,\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\n            'success': trial['success']\n        }\n        if not trial['success']:\n            failures[trial['signature']] = entry\n        else:\n            successes.append(entry)\n    failure_list = list(failures.values())\n    failure_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\n    success_list = successes[:]\n    success_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\n    combined = failure_list + success_list\n    retained = combined[:capacity]\n    retained.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\n    memory = retained\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in retained]\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}\n\nNow escape: Replace \\ with \\\\, \" with \\\", newline with \\n.\n\nBut note: In JSON, we need to embed the Python code as a string. So the string will contain newlines as \\n. When the JSON is parsed, the string will have actual newlines.\n\nWe'll construct the string with triple quotes in the Python code? Actually the code is a string literal in the JSON, so we need to escape it accordingly.\n\nLet's write the response JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    capacity = case['capacity']\\n    trials = case['trials']\\n    failures = {}\\n    successes = []\\n    for i, trial in enumerate(trials):\\n        entry = {\\n            'last_trial': i,\\n            'reflection': trial['reflection'],\\n            'signature': trial['signature'],\\n            'success': trial['success']\\n        }\\n        if not trial['success']:\\n            failures[trial['signature']] = entry\\n        else:\\n            successes.append(entry)\\n    failure_list = list(failures.values())\\n    failure_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\\n    success_list = successes[:]\\n    success_list.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'], reverse=True)\\n    combined = failure_list + success_list\\n    retained = combined[:capacity]\\n    retained.sort(key=lambda e: e['last_trial'])\\n    memory = retained\\n    signatures = [e['signature'] for e in retained]\\n    return {'memory': memory, 'signatures': signatures}\\n\"\n}\n\nBut note: The last line has no newline at end? The code ends with return statement then newline? We'll include newline before closing quote.\n\nWe must ensure the JSON is valid: no trailing commas, quotes properly escaped.\n\nAlso note: In the code, we use single quotes for dict keys. That's fine because we are inside a double-quoted JSON string. Single quotes don't need escaping in JSON strings. But we have backslashes